# exp through a table (Tang's reduction): x = k ln2/N + r, exp x =
# 2^(k/N) (1 + q(r)), the table 2^(i/N) read at k's low bits, the high
# ones added to its exponent field.
# f64 under gt1030: the table's one load buys a degree 4 polynomial for
# degree 11 (335.625 against 553.375, both within 2 ulp).
approx exp(x) in -700..700 as f64 ulp 2 nosplit norational free list 2
# f32: the table meets the target too, but its load costs more than the
# terms it saves (19.875 against 15).
approx exp(x) in -87..88 ulp 2 nosplit norational free list 2
# without an fma, k ln2/N in three Cody-Waite parts: k has 14 bits
approx exp(x) in -87..88 nofma ulp 2 nosplit norational free list 2
